\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Majorana-to-Dirac cross-section ratio $R(v) = \sigma _T^{\rm  Maj}/\sigma _T^{\rm  Dir}$ as a function of relative velocity. \textbf  {Top}: For the MAP benchmark ($\lambda = 48.6$, green), $R$ rises from $\sim 0.5$ at low velocity to a peak of $R = 1.094$ at $v \approx 53$~km/s, entering an $R > 1$ zone (shaded) from 37 to 2000~km/s. BP1 ($\lambda = 1.9$, blue) remains below unity. \textbf  {Bottom}: The logarithmic derivative $dR/d\ln v$ shows the extremum at $v \approx 53$~km/s --- the velocity where intermediate dSphs should exhibit the Majorana fingerprint. This feature is the unique experimental signature of Majorana spin statistics in the self-interaction channel.}}{22}{figure.80}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:majorana_dirac}{{12}{22}{Majorana-to-Dirac cross-section ratio $R(v) = \sigma _T^{\rm Maj}/\sigma _T^{\rm Dir}$ as a function of relative velocity. \textbf {Top}: For the MAP benchmark ($\lambda = 48.6$, green), $R$ rises from $\sim 0.5$ at low velocity to a peak of $R = 1.094$ at $v \approx 53$~km/s, entering an $R > 1$ zone (shaded) from 37 to 2000~km/s. BP1 ($\lambda = 1.9$, blue) remains below unity. \textbf {Bottom}: The logarithmic derivative $dR/d\ln v$ shows the extremum at $v \approx 53$~km/s --- the velocity where intermediate dSphs should exhibit the Majorana fingerprint. This feature is the unique experimental signature of Majorana spin statistics in the self-interaction channel}{figure.80}{}}
